WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.754 --> 00:00:03.921
(audience applauding)

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.210
- Thank you, welcome,
welcome, everyone, welcome.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.980
Welcome, transfer students,
visiting international students,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.768
Eli Whitney students, and
welcome, first year students.

00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:22.935
(audience applauding)

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.210
So happy to see you all after
a fantastic move in weekend,

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.370
you all brought the best
weather in many years,

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.120
so I think you're off to a
great start to your four years.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.411
Did you enjoy Bulldog Bash?

00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:42.411
(audience cheering)

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.610
We should do it again next year.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.490
Okay, good, okay, thanks.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.450
Yeah, and really hope
you're all getting settled

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.060
and excited for the year ahead.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.790
Classes start tomorrow morning.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.890
And one thing you're gonna find out about

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.940
classes around here is
that you have some time

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.680
to pick what you're gonna take,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.460
we have this shopping period.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.670
And to try out the courses
you wanna take first.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.690
And of course, that's
what I hope you'll do.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.220
It's a really exciting
time, a little hectic.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.870
But a lot of it's about
juggling a lot of the options

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.170
and choices, great choices that you have,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.100
as you make up your minds.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.390
But before any of that
happens, today, this afternoon,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.250
we are gonna have this opportunity

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.470
to think in the broadest sense

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.380
about the intellectual
community that you're joining.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.870
And to start thinking about your own

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.260
intellectual life here at Yale.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.660
Some of you may have
planned out every semester

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.570
for your entire four years ahead,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.820
down to every class you will take.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.030
While some of you are just the opposite,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.970
not knowing even where to start.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.720
And of course, that's all good.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.770
And many of you are probably
somewhere in between

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.380
with a general idea of what
your next move will be,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.720
but with an openness to new ideas.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:01:59.970
So whatever you have in mind,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.480
I hope this afternoon
that you will get a sense

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.913
of the world of ideas
that are awaiting you.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.230
And there's also some
practical business today

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:10.220
that we'll attend to later on,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.640
which is learning how to
sing "Bright College Years."

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.680
And so with help from the Glee Club

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.640
which is with us here today,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.650
you'll get a chance to practice,

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.410
and then by the time you leave this place,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.700
you'll be able to take your
place besides Yale students

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.800
and alumni who know this
song inside and out.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.420
And you have the lyrics with
you as part of your program,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.763
and we'll come back to
this after our speech.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.310
So it's now my pleasure to introduce

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.630
our keynote speaker, Sarah Demers,

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:45.740
the Horace D. Taft Associate
Professor of Physics.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.040
Professor Demers is an award winning

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.430
distinguished member of the Yale faculty,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.950
and an excellent example

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:55.480
of the intellectual
possibilities ahead of you.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.970
After receiving her AB
from Harvard University,

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.230
Professor Demers attended
the University of Rochester,

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.480
Harvard is a good school.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.000
And so is University of Rochester.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.930
And where she was awarded
her MA and PhD in physics.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.310
After teaching at Roberts Wesleyan College

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.270
and serving as a research associate

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:19.090
at Stanford's Linear Accelerator Center,

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:22.340
Professor Demers joined
the Yale faculty in 2009

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.610
as Assistant Professor of Physics.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.970
And in the spring of 2020,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.450
she'll be singing, teaching
a course, did I say sing?

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.683
She'll be teaching a course
titled The Physics of Music.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.460
Professor Demers researches physics

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.850
beyond the standard model at
CERN's Large Hadron Collider,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:46.020
and is currently studying
Higgs boson and Z boson decays.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.470
She also works on the trigger
experiment for the study.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.430
In addition, she leads
the Demers Group at Yale,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.360
co-leads the ATLAS Upgrade Physics Group,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.850
becomes the elected Chair

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.140
of the US Atlas Institute
Board in January,

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.023
and is a member of the Mu2e
collaboration at Fermilab.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.090
Professor Demers is the
cofounder of the Yale Franke

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.460
and Whitney Humanities Center Group,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.520
Interdisciplinary Arts
and Science Research

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.920
with Associate Professor
of Theater Studies

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.393
and Director of the Dance
Studies curriculum, Emily Coates.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.720
She also collaborates
with Professor Coates

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.420
on a project that bridges
physics and dance,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.850
and they co-teach a course
called The Physics of Dance,

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.797
and co-wrote a book called
"Physics and Dance."

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:34.690
That's really cool, isn't she?

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.010
So as you think about the journeys

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.340
you're about to start tomorrow,

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:40.130
you cannot have a better
guide than Professor Demers.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.245
Please join me in welcoming her.

00:04:43.245 --> 00:04:46.412
(audience applauding)

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.210
- Thank you, Dean Chun,

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:59.480
and welcome to each and every one of you.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.280
I am really grateful for the chance

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.290
to pause with you this afternoon

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.970
before starting in with the academic year

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.610
that stretches out in front of us.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.210
I'm only one of many people
at Yale who cares about you.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.003
We're excited about what you
bring to this institution.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.130
We're in anticipation of
what we'll learn from you.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.220
And we have the highest expectations

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.130
for what you will gain while you're here

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:31.563
and what you'll take away.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:33.973
But no pressure.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.163
You're here on purpose, you
just have to do your best.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.153
So, what's your plan?

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.150
How are you going to navigate

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.253
the resources that surround you?

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.690
I've been here for about a
decade, a decade and two months.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:59.460
And I'm still coming to terms
with what we have access to.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.610
Research tools, galleries, studios,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:06.643
machine shops, libraries,
it's overwhelming.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.943
Say about the experts
who populate this space.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:15.300
In our time this afternoon,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.960
I have two themes that I
want to explore with you,

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.280
and I'll end with a suggestion.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.280
The two themes are balance
and, obviously, napkins.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.393
The suggestion has to
do with your questions.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.830
And before I get started,
I just wanna note

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.580
I'm speaking to you today as
a member of this community,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.750
as a faculty member, as a physicist.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.160
I'm not a dean, I'm not a medical doctor,

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.720
I'm not currently your professor,

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.280
and I'm one perspective of many,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.880
so you should keep that in mind.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.650
So, how are you going to choose

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.803
from our thousands of courses?

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:58.193
Balance is a reasonable goal.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:01.580
How will you balance your coursework

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:04.410
with the hundreds of
extracurricular activities

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.463
I'm sure you have already signed up for?

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.280
How are you gonna balance
sustaining your connections

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.193
from home while building
your networks here?

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.750
How are you gonna balance
pursuing what you know you love

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:21.423
with discovering what you could love?

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:25.560
And this is related, but
it's a little different.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.150
How are you gonna balance doubling down on

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.660
what you're good at with training yourself

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.653
in new ways and becoming
an expert in something new?

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.540
How are you gonna
balance your inward focus

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.010
on your education and your college time

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.720
with an outward focus on our community

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.873
in New Haven and beyond?

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:51.260
Will you be truly advanced, above average,

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.993
and actually get enough sleep?

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:58.493
Consider it, you're worth it.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.870
As you do your calculations
and make your decisions,

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:06.310
balanced seems like a
reasonable guiding principle.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:08.630
And it's come more and more into fashion.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:11.700
Work life balance, that idea has resulted

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.823
in important societal
changes, positive changes.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.640
I spent a lot of time
thinking about balance

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.670
in a lot of different contexts.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:22.793
And do you know what?

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.483
I actually think that
it can miss the point.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:29.430
And before I defend that statement,

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.620
let me introduce some physics.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:32.970
You didn't think you were gonna get away

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.370
without some physics?

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.870
Okay, so what are the
conditions for balance?

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.400
How does a physical system stay balanced?

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:46.140
The center of mass has to
be over an area of support.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.580
If your center of mass comes out

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.210
away from your area of
support, you start to fall.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:53.950
So, what is an area of support?

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:56.920
Well, you take the
contact that you're making

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.720
with the ground, in my case,
right now it's my two feet.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.370
And then it includes that area of contact

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.370
with the area that spans it,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.820
meaning the rectangle
between my two feet, okay?

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.770
So the wider my stance, the
bigger my area of support.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:16.140
As I go down to one foot,
which you can't see,

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.893
but I promise I'm doing, briefly,

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.990
my area of support is very constrained.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:26.510
If I go up on my toes,
I have very little room

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.633
for my center of mass to
wiggle before I start to fall.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.910
And let's stick to a concrete

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:34.823
physical system here for a while.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.543
My challenge with balance as a goal.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:43.733
You can be balanced
under a tremendous load,

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.023
and you also can be
balancing and exhausted.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:53.790
That's why when people ask
me, as they frequently do,

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:58.790
about my work life
balance, I tell them, "No."

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.190
I hear that question and I
imagine walking on a tightrope

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:05.720
with my work in one outstretched arm

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:09.010
and my life in the other,
whatever that means.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.530
And I imagine if I'm gonna
add a little more work,

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:14.770
I have to add a little more life.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.940
And the next thing you know,

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:18.960
you find yourself at three
o'clock in the morning

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.240
making cookies for your kids' classrooms

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.570
because you agreed to serve
on a committee for Dean Chun,

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.470
it is the way of madness, that balance.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.703
I love those committees, I do.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.450
But my goal is not to balance,

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.070
as positive and important
as balance can be.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.250
My goal is to thrive.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:46.053
And that's what I hope
for you here at Yale.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:49.240
So how do we thrive?

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:53.850
We have to know who we are
and who we wanna become.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.040
We have to know who we love, who loves us.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.093
We have to understand what
sustains us and what drains us.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:05.300
I want to be centered, and
I think that's the point.

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:06.700
And if it means that I spend

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:10.220
some time balanced, then fantastic.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.430
When I'm centered, I don't have to remain

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.290
in my basketball defensive
stance that I learned,

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.530
I actually can go up on
point when I have to.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.880
And I can move my center of mass out

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.780
in front of my source of support.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.750
You know what that is, when you
move your center of mass out

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:28.710
in front of your source of support?

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.540
It's called walking.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.090
You fall and catch yourself, right?

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:36.013
That's what moving is,
it's falling and catching.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:39.340
When you're centered, you can do that,

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:42.930
and I recommend that over balanced.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:46.363
And I suggest thriving over reacting.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:51.080
I have one last comment on that topic

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.870
before we move on to napkins,

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:54.470
which I know you're waiting for.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.190
If you wanna be centered,

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:02.220
you actually have to
know what your center is.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.380
You need time to reflect.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:08.630
And the most common mistake
we can make in this context

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:12.533
is giving short shrift to
thinking and to sleeping.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:15.446
And you know what that means.

00:12:15.446 --> 00:12:19.053
It means that you have to turn
off your phone and reflect.

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:22.663
So now for napkins.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.700
I grew up, kindergarten
through eighth grade

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:30.040
in a small town in
Vermont, Groton, Vermont,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.930
a town of about 600.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.770
My father is a United Methodist pastor.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:38.520
He originally trained as a pianist,

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.490
and my mother studied art
history and education.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:45.200
And I'm convinced that the
only job my mother can't do

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.290
is the one that she
explicitly trained for.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:51.650
She lasted about a day in a
middle school teaching art

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.720
before she decided no.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.980
But she's been a milk
tester for dairy farms,

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.340
she's worked in a nursing
home, done hospice care,

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.060
run a chamber of commerce,
been a telemarketer,

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.290
run a shelter for victims
of domestic violence,

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.500
run a homeless shelter, et cetera.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.240
If there's a complicated job

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.070
that doesn't involve getting 25 students

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.733
to simultaneously paint, call my mother.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:22.040
I didn't grow up in a wealthy
or a formal household.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.900
And the goal that I was raised with

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.493
was not to do well in the
world, but to do good.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.720
As I was entering high school, we moved

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.090
to the booming metropolis
of Morrisville, Vermont,

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:38.900
over 1000 people, and we
had our own movie theater.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:42.273
One screen, but our own theater.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:46.020
There were AP classes in my school,

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:49.350
advanced placement classes,
yeah, for college credit.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.920
And the person who taught
my AP calculus class

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.510
was my AP English teacher.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:59.430
And we started calculus
with the quote of the day.

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.490
And I'll never forget that
quote of the day discussion

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:06.170
stretching into half of the course period.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.340
And getting to the AP test

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:13.840
and not recognizing those curly S things

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.440
that some of you may know as integrals,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.700
and if you don't know
that, come talk to me,

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:20.730
I'd love to talk to you about calculus,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:22.993
so that didn't go necessarily well.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.710
I was an extremely ambitious kid,

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:28.550
so I really had run out of classes

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.223
by the end of my junior year.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:33.880
And as has already been confessed,

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.440
I was not as enlightened as all of you,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.653
at that point, I applied to Harvard.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.110
Can get that out if you have to.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.260
It was a year early from a small school

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:45.460
that nobody had heard of.

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.640
I knew that they were need blind,

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.343
so my family could afford it.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.550
And I had multiple alumni interviews.

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:56.470
In an incredible twist of fate,

00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:58.873
my final alumni interviewer,

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:03.540
her children had been my dad's students

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:06.700
when he was a music teacher
before going to seminary.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:09.670
She was compassionate and connected.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.740
And she knew that I wasn't
getting into Harvard

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:14.320
from a school that no one
had heard of a year early

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.370
with marginal accomplishments.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:19.800
She asked me if I'd ever
considered private school,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:21.080
and I told her it wasn't an option

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.390
because we couldn't afford it.

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.280
But she knew about
scholarship opportunities,

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.840
and she set me up with an interview

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:29.050
at Philips Academy in Andover,

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.313
and I ended up doing my senior year there.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.470
There's one experience in particular

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.010
that has stuck with me from Andover.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.200
I can't remember why,

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.450
but I didn't go home for Thanksgiving.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.900
So I was invited to the head of school's

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.423
house for Thanksgiving.

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:51.480
And I remember the room, walking in,

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:56.040
and the dark wood paneling everywhere.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.160
But mostly, I remember the place settings.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.430
I don't know how you feel
about place settings.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:05.400
I was trained on place
settings, it was one of my jobs.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.900
Plate in the center, fork on the left,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.233
knife and spoon on the right,
napkin folded under the fork.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:15.410
That's how we did it
when I was growing up.

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:17.750
This place setting had multiple knives,

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.380
multiple spoons, multiple forks.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:22.380
It had things to the left of the plate,

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:23.930
to the right of the plate,

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.310
and then the indignity of it,

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:30.240
stretching on top of the
plate, moving away from me.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.100
Most alarming from my perspective

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:36.930
was the construction on top of my plate.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:40.940
It was a 3D conglomeration
that I recognized

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:45.500
as my napkin exactly where
the food is supposed to go.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.580
I knew I had to be on point.

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:50.060
There were rules.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:52.170
Nobody was talking about them,

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.070
and I was gonna have to pay attention.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:58.460
I looked up just to see

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.893
that every napkin was gone except my own.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.010
All napkins missing.

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:07.630
And it was one of those
moments where you realize

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:10.823
something here is not like the other.

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.250
It probably only took me
a fraction of a second

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.360
to see those napkins on people's laps.

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:19.793
I remember thinking,

00:17:20.627 --> 00:17:24.267
"I do not understand, what is the purpose,

00:17:24.267 --> 00:17:27.660
"what is the utility of
the napkin on the lap?"

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:31.500
Since then, it's saved me
many times from spills.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:33.860
But at the time, I thought that
was a little bit ridiculous,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.323
but I was going with the flow,
my napkin went on my lap.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.290
This idea that there are rules,

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.480
and it's obvious that there are rules,

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.160
but no one is talking about them

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.100
and you don't know what they are.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.813
I call those things napkin moments.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.133
And I've had many of them.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.580
After Andover, I was accepted to Harvard.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:00.320
I didn't have a lot of time
to figure out a summer job

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:04.150
between high school and
going back before college,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.410
so I took on two positions.

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.970
I was a maid at a resort in Stowe,

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:11.680
and I was maintaining tennis courts,

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:15.670
which required spreading
a lot of chemicals.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.820
And it's likely that exposure to chemicals

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:19.610
in one of those two contexts

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.360
landed me with an autoimmune disease.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.010
So the week that I was
supposed to be at Harvard

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:28.560
for orientation, I was having surgery,

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:31.020
and I had to miss the year.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:33.700
You're well placed to think about this.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:35.860
At Harvard in those days,
and maybe it's true,

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:37.170
I don't know if it's true here.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:40.260
If you miss orientation,
you miss the year,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.640
they say, "Come back in a year."

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.937
I remember going to orientation
the next year and thinking,

00:18:45.937 --> 00:18:49.962
"They have overestimated how
valuable this experience is."

00:18:49.962 --> 00:18:51.250
(audience laughing)

00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:54.093
I'm sure you don't feel
that way right now.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:58.660
Anyway, it was ironic,

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:01.750
the kid who wants to go
to college a year early

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:03.740
ends up going a year late,

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:05.520
but it gave me the gift of arriving

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:07.023
to college fairly centered.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:10.380
And my napkin moments
continued in college.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:13.613
They weren't only social,
they were also academic.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:18.500
I remember my first physics
classroom at Harvard,

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:22.030
walking in, and there
was a cluster of people,

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:23.910
it was a reunion, and they were

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.800
talking about the Physics Olympics.

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:29.867
And I remember thinking, "Isn't that cute?

00:19:29.867 --> 00:19:33.330
"Physics Olympics, like the
Olympics except for physics."

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:37.437
And I realized, "Oh, that is a real thing.

00:19:37.437 --> 00:19:41.000
"I'm probably in the wrong
classroom right now."

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:42.510
And it turns out that I was,

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:44.150
there was shopping period there as well,

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:45.773
I was able to adjust.

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:50.170
I took an astronomy class
with about a dozen students

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:52.990
and I was the only woman in the class.

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:55.670
Our professor spent a
lot of time communing

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:58.860
with the blackboard, which I understand.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.800
But he did occasionally turn
around and interact with us.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.730
We had a ritual once a week

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:06.070
when he would hand back our homework.

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:10.567
And he would read off our
names one at a time, "Daniel,"

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:15.297
and he would scan the room, and
Daniel would raise his hand,

00:20:15.297 --> 00:20:19.180
"James," and, "Jonathan,"

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:21.717
and he would inevitably get to, "Sarah."

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.397
My classmates begged me,

00:20:24.397 --> 00:20:27.760
"Please don't raise your
hand, see what he does."

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:31.290
Will he actually connect
the one female sounding name

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.880
with the one female
looking person in the room

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:36.260
who has consistently
raised her hand to Sarah?

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.980
But of course, I didn't wanna
make it any more uncomfortable

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:42.050
in those moments than it
already was scanning the room.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.570
So I didn't really pause.

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:45.920
And it's never been clear to me

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:47.970
if he just was really a warrior

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:52.970
of trying to not see gender,
he was helping me out,

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:55.290
or if he really was that oblivious,

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:56.823
I think he might have been.

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:00.680
The problem sets in that class

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.970
were actually my true napkin moments.

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:05.560
I remember reading the problems,

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:07.880
and they went something like this.

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:10.370
You have designed and constructed

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:15.370
a 250 kilogram rocket and
launched it toward Mars.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:19.420
How long will its journey take?

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:23.343
Hint, take into account
the rotation of the earth.

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.700
I'm sure that there was
more information provided,

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:31.440
but that's about the
level that I was getting.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:32.707
And I remember thinking to myself,

00:21:32.707 --> 00:21:34.747
"I'm not just missing something,

00:21:34.747 --> 00:21:37.330
"I think I'm missing everything."

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.793
And I don't know if you've
had that experience.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:41.973
It's interesting.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:49.470
There were rules in lots
of different instances,

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.010
and I didn't know them.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.200
One last example from a philosophy class

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:55.940
where there's a very special way

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:59.380
to write a philosophy
paper, I remember writing,

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:03.850
making argument X eight
times in three different ways

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:07.033
and getting the feedback,
you haven't made argument X.

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:11.460
In spite of really dedicated teachers

00:22:11.460 --> 00:22:13.640
and a year at Andover, I was underprepared

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:17.423
and confused in physics
throughout much of college.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:22.410
And there are three things
that I want to acknowledge

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:25.330
while we're together about napkin moments.

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:27.040
The first thing to acknowledge is

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:29.640
that they really can be exhausting.

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:32.410
If you're at a place that
feels like a different planet,

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.290
it's gonna take some energy.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:35.810
Anyone who's lived in a foreign country

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:37.660
knows what this feels like,

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:40.560
and we have international
students with us here.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:42.010
We have non-traditional students,

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:43.560
veterans who are returning to school,

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:44.870
we have transfer students,

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:47.513
we have a lot of first
generation students.

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.540
Many of us are gonna continue
to have napkin moments

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:52.990
over the course of this year.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.550
The second thing to point out

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:58.900
is that they can be productive.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:02.660
If you find yourself comfortable

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:05.353
to the point where you're
not being challenged,

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:09.320
make sure you seek out those opportunities

00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:12.090
to be productively uncomfortable.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:13.870
I'll give you an example.

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:16.310
I am productively uncomfortable

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:20.060
when I co-teach physics of
dance with Professor Coates,

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:23.590
and we're lined up on one
side of the dance studio.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:26.620
And she tells us to make
our way to the other side,

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:30.570
giving us some kind of an
improvisational prompt.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:35.570
That is terror, but
productive terror for me.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:37.810
And I encourage you to seek out ways

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:39.660
to be challenged if you haven't been.

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:44.130
The last thing that I want to say

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:49.130
is to keep in mind that when
you feel the least equipped

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:52.520
and like you least belong,
those times might be

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.423
the very times when you
have the most to give.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.320
In spite of my hiccups
with the philosophy papers,

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:00.970
which were substantial,

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:03.303
I considered myself a humanities person.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:07.470
Physics challenged me and thrilled me

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:10.280
From the moment I learned
about coordinate systems,

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:12.980
which is kind of embarrassing
to admit, but it's true.

00:24:14.410 --> 00:24:16.780
And it took me many, many
years to get to a point

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.180
where I was thinking like a physicist.

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:22.310
Now it's really second nature
to me, and my other talents

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:25.370
and inclinations have
come along for the ride,

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.120
and it makes me a better physicist.

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:32.840
The last thing I wanna talk about,

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:36.050
I know that it's warm,
you're all doing well.

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:37.453
There's more physics coming.

00:24:38.770 --> 00:24:41.460
The last thing I wanna talk
about is your questions.

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.790
Because I have an organizational principle

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:46.270
to suggest for your time here.

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:48.490
It's not gonna help
you choose your courses

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:51.110
or your extracurricular activities,

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:54.190
but it might give you a
new way to pay attention

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:57.040
and make the most of the
situations you find yourself in.

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:59.830
It's not a new idea.

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:01.760
It really crystallized
for me when I was reading

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:04.260
The Chronicle of Higher Education.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:07.053
Do you know about
productive procrastination?

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:10.033
It's a good one to figure out.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:13.200
You shouldn't procrastinate.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:15.050
But when you do procrastinate,

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:16.640
how much better to have
accomplished something

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:19.780
you're proud of in addition
to the guilt that you feel

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:22.730
and the problems that you've
dug yourself deeper in?

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:26.190
So for me, I read The
Chronicle of Higher Education

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.500
to procrastinate, and in 2013,

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:31.130
there was an article by James Lang

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:35.520
that referenced a book about
successful college students,

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:37.717
written by Ken Bain,

00:25:37.717 --> 00:25:41.130
"What the Best College Students Do."

00:25:41.130 --> 00:25:44.800
I don't often put much stock
in those kinds of books,

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:48.900
but Ken Bain wrote a very insightful book

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:53.890
about teaching and what it is
to become a better teacher,

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:55.320
so I was optimistic,

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.990
and there were a lot
of good ideas in there.

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:00.160
The one that stuck with me is

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:02.973
identify your questions,

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.803
and carry your questions
with you everywhere.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:11.643
So, what are your questions?

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:17.183
What is it that you as an
individual wonder about?

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:19.383
What do you worry about?

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:21.960
It could be big questions,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:23.900
questions like how do we create

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:26.800
and maintain justice in a society?

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:31.010
Or, how can I use my privilege
to open doors for others?

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:34.333
Or, how do we get the
plastics out of the ocean?

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:40.003
Identify your most urgent
questions and carry them with you.

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.810
If you are thinking about justice,

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:46.580
assume that your math
class, your painting course,

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:49.190
and your time playing intramural soccer

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:50.840
all have something to contribute.

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:54.150
It's a way for you to take responsibility

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.480
and some level of direction
for your education,

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:00.640
and let you, with your unique skills,

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:03.430
background, perspective, make connections

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:06.523
and discoveries that otherwise
wouldn't be possible.

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:12.440
Like all advice, this is
potentially dangerous.

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.390
What I do not mean by this

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:18.120
is for you to walk into your
classes, ignore the syllabi,

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:21.063
and bend the course to your own will.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:24.160
You're at a research university,

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:27.480
so your faculty are
theorists and practitioners

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.020
who are immersed in our disciplines.

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:32.100
We bring our own goals and discipline

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:34.320
specific questions to the table,

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:36.030
and we're well informed.

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:38.230
Your own questions are a supplement.

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:40.890
They aren't an excuse to stop listening,

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:43.243
they're a motivation to listen well.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.850
The assumption with that kind of listening

00:27:49.850 --> 00:27:51.980
is that the information is relevant

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:54.560
and you could learn
something transformative.

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:55.960
And they actually enable you

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:58.143
to contribute in unexpected ways.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:01.840
What I wanna do with our remaining time

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:04.090
is walk you through
just one of my questions

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:06.250
and share some of what I've learned

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:08.890
by this way of interacting with the world.

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.090
So, here's the question.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:13.923
Why does the universe have a speed limit?

00:28:16.870 --> 00:28:18.713
I'm fascinated by velocity.

00:28:19.950 --> 00:28:22.173
How fast are we going right now?

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:26.860
We don't appear to be moving at all,

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.280
but we're falling around
the sun at between

00:28:30.329 --> 00:28:33.460
15 to 20 miles per second.

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:36.383
It's very peaceful, if warm.

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:40.140
We aren't near the center of the galaxy,

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.500
which is actually for the
best, because we think

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:46.140
there's a massive black hole
at the center of the galaxy.

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.540
We're out in the spiral of the Milky Way,

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:50.040
and it takes our solar system

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.070
a few hundred million
miles to complete an orbit.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:57.193
That means that we're moving
at about 150 miles per second.

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:01.460
Our galaxy moves throughout
the local group of galaxies,

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:03.770
and if we think even bigger,

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:06.160
our universe appears to be in a period

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:08.223
of accelerating expansion.

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:12.550
In spite of all of this relative motion,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:16.920
we will not, it seems,
find a pair of objects

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:19.050
that are moving faster
than the speed of light

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:20.400
with respect to each other.

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:23.563
Our universe has a speed limit.

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:27.340
We're gonna dive in one more step,

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:29.140
because I'm a particle physicist

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:31.563
and you're Yale students, we can do this.

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:35.820
Thinking about fundamental particles,

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:39.210
we have a few examples without mass.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:42.100
Photons are particles of light,

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:45.210
gluons, they're involved
in the strong force,

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.250
quantum chromodynamics.

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:51.523
And we've theorized gravitons,
mediators of gravity.

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:55.690
Those particles have no mass,

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:58.583
and by definition, only
move at the speed of light.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:03.840
If you have a particle with
even a little bit of mass,

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:06.543
you can't get that particle
to the speed of light.

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:11.130
Which means that velocity is
intrinsically tied to mass.

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:15.070
And the mechanism of
mass discovered in 2012

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:18.410
is the Higgs field, something
that I'm studying right now,

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:21.943
which ties into one of
my fundamental questions.

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:25.330
Without the Higgs field,
elementary particles

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:27.970
would be zooming around
at the speed of light.

00:30:27.970 --> 00:30:30.940
So my question about the
speed limit of the universe

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:33.493
is really linked to the question of mass.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:39.630
In 2012, reasonably soon after
the discovery of the Higgs

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:41.910
by my collaboration and another,

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:45.090
I was with a few hundred
of my very good friends,

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.510
we were still celebrating the discovery.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:49.250
And at the back of the auditorium,

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:51.220
I ran into a colleague of mine

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:55.310
who is a really talented
science communicator.

00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:59.980
I asked him, "So, how's it
going, talking about the Higgs?"

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:04.980
And he said, "It's hard, it's
really difficult to explain.

00:31:05.577 --> 00:31:07.827
"In a public setting, you don't pull out

00:31:07.827 --> 00:31:10.067
"quantum mechanics and field theory."

00:31:11.250 --> 00:31:14.630
And I agreed, it was a challenge for us.

00:31:14.630 --> 00:31:16.113
So I looked for help.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:21.120
I collaborate, as you've heard,
with Professor Emily Coates.

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:22.610
There was a grant that was being offered

00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:25.500
by the New Haven Council
for the Arts to support

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:29.100
artist scientist teams,
it was called Reintegrate,

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:30.950
and it was trying to pull pairs

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:33.160
of artists and scientists together.

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:35.900
And Emily and I were
looking for something to do

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:37.817
associated with it, and I asked her,

00:31:37.817 --> 00:31:39.947
"Can we tackle the Higgs boson for this?"

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:43.280
In the process of our work together,

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:46.210
Emily went to one of my
public lectures on the Higgs.

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.047
And she asked me after,

00:31:48.047 --> 00:31:49.907
"Do you have any idea what you're doing

00:31:49.907 --> 00:31:51.987
"when you're talking up there?

00:31:51.987 --> 00:31:54.707
"You're making all kinds of motions,

00:31:54.707 --> 00:31:58.307
"you're basically dancing the
Higgs as you're up there."

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:00.747
And I said, "No, I had no idea,

00:32:00.747 --> 00:32:02.357
"and it's problematic to hear that

00:32:02.357 --> 00:32:03.727
"because I'm gonna be self conscious

00:32:03.727 --> 00:32:06.740
"for the rest of my life now
talking about the Higgs boson."

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:10.400
And she asked me, "Do you know
if your colleagues do this?"

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:12.600
And I said, "I have no idea."

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:16.140
So we set off to CERN to
interview some of my colleagues.

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:20.270
And we asked them, "Describe the Higgs,"

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:22.110
and we collected their analogies.

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:26.000
And we watched how their
interactions of their bodies

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:28.863
actually mapped to the
words they were using.

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:33.140
In this process, I thought very deeply

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:35.440
about the ways that I was imagining

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.680
the Higgs mechanism to work.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:40.093
As I talked about it,
as I thought about it.

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:45.233
And noticed the pieces that weren't safe,

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:48.860
places where I'd lifted
in macroscopic ideas

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:51.440
to describe a microscopic phenomenon.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:53.614
And it forced me to go back and look

00:32:53.614 --> 00:32:56.623
more deeply, more carefully at the theory.

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.390
It's the people who
understand the concepts

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:02.590
that are really the best place to come up

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.393
with a safe analogy, right?

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.700
The moral of this story for me is that

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:11.100
we shouldn't narrow ourselves prematurely

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:12.853
when it comes to our questions.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:16.970
If you care about getting
plastics out of the ocean,

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:21.970
don't take only courses on
plastics and courses on oceans.

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:24.380
I learned more about mass

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:25.940
through working with a choreographer

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:27.940
than I ever could have imagined I would.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:31.240
And I know this may
seem like a selfish way

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:33.140
to approach the liberal arts.

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:36.380
It's basically looking
around with your questions

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:38.157
and thinking, "All of these things

00:33:38.157 --> 00:33:41.090
"can teach me about my stuff."

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:42.900
But I really think it's
the best compliment

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:45.237
you can pay another discipline, to say,

00:33:45.237 --> 00:33:48.457
"I believe that you're
relevant to what I care about,

00:33:48.457 --> 00:33:50.787
"and there are insights
that can be offered

00:33:50.787 --> 00:33:53.137
"that are only limited by my ignorance,

00:33:53.137 --> 00:33:56.450
"and I can't even imagine
the connections yet."

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:00.100
And your stuff and your questions

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:02.540
will change in your time here.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:05.410
I know that you will discover questions

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:07.360
if you're paying attention.

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.070
Conversations with each other,

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:11.540
inside and outside of the classroom.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:14.540
And I hope and assume that
some of your questions

00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:17.970
are gonna evolve through
your interactions,

00:34:17.970 --> 00:34:21.053
again, with each other inside
and outside of the classroom.

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:26.780
I'm gonna free us all soon,

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:30.093
because I know we need time
to sleep and time to think.

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:33.480
Just a few more words.

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:36.900
Balance, I think it's a useful framework,

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:39.900
but it misses something
that I think is critical.

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:42.240
I really want you all to thrive here,

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:44.633
and that can occasionally require focus.

00:34:46.220 --> 00:34:49.280
Good luck with your napkin moments,

00:34:49.280 --> 00:34:51.223
you're actually not alone.

00:34:52.890 --> 00:34:55.900
Remember, define your questions

00:34:55.900 --> 00:34:58.450
and carry them with you everywhere.

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:02.720
At this point, we're not here
for your accomplishments,

00:35:02.720 --> 00:35:04.253
we're here for your potential.

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:08.083
And one last analogy.

00:35:08.970 --> 00:35:13.970
This institution is not a
fancy Thanksgiving dinner,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:17.860
and the course catalog is not a menu.

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:22.120
You aren't surrounded by
assembled dishes here,

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:24.253
you're surrounded by ingredients.

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:27.963
Because you don't consume an education,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:31.290
you have to construct it.

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:36.290
So, turn off your phone, get
some sleep, do your best,

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:40.700
find the help when you need it.

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:45.350
And when you can, embrace
the challenges you face

00:35:45.350 --> 00:35:48.520
that train your brain in productive ways

00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:50.893
to access the world in different ways.

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.740
Thank you very much for your time

00:35:53.740 --> 00:35:55.100
and attention this afternoon,

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:59.069
and I really look forward
to seeing you around campus.

00:35:59.069 --> 00:36:02.236
(audience applauding)

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:19.410
- So, Professor Demers,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:22.700
thank you for that
amazing, inspiring words.

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:27.380
And I hope you all will
take her advice to,

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:30.500
as you get to know each other
over the next four years,

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.160
share your napkin moments with each other,

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:37.550
share your questions and
help each other find balance.

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:39.090
You're gonna have a big reception

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:43.000
in your residential colleges
followed by a formal dinner,

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.890
a Thanksgiving-like
dinner in the dining hall.

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:49.111
It's really a memorable
event and a great way

00:36:49.111 --> 00:36:53.980
to spend the evening before
your first day of classes here.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:55.190
So, I hope you look forward to that,

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:58.900
and you all look really wonderful
and handsome and lovely.

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:01.030
But before we leave today, as promised,

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:02.440
you're gonna learn one of our

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:04.750
really most beautiful traditions.

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:08.580
And so we have the world
renowned Yale Glee Club

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:12.760
with us here together with our
most distinguished director,

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:16.560
Jeff Douma, who's going to teach
you "Bright College Years,"

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:18.663
so please give a warm welcome.

00:37:18.663 --> 00:37:21.830
(audience applauding)

00:37:24.680 --> 00:37:26.150
- Thank you, Dean Chun.

00:37:26.150 --> 00:37:27.500
Hi, everyone, good afternoon.

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:29.900
As Dean Chun explained,
we conclude the address

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:32.610
by inviting all of you to join in

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.040
a time honored Yale College tradition,

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:37.630
the singing of our alma
mater, "Bright College Years."

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:41.100
I have a few members of the Yale Glee Club

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:42.610
here to help me out.

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:46.380
They're gonna sing through
verse one once for you

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:48.170
so you can hear what it sounds like.

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:51.420
And after that, I will
invite all of you to stand

00:37:51.420 --> 00:37:54.270
and to sing verses one and two together

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:57.340
as they are printed on
the back of the program.

00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.670
It's our tradition at the
end of the second verse

00:38:00.670 --> 00:38:03.280
to wave our handkerchiefs in the air,

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:06.540
so be ready for that and follow our lead.

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:10.793
Sing out loud, sing out
strong, and welcome to Yale.

